<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.3" snapToGrid="false">
  <code><![CDATA[turtles-own [
  score
  played?
  original-color
]


;; Observer procedures

to setup
  clear-all
  ask patches [ set pcolor white ]
  populate
  reset-ticks
end

to go
  if ticks >= 2000 or count turtles = 0 [ stop ]

  clear-participation
  let players turtles with [
    count turtles-on neighbors4 > 0
  ]
  ask players [ play ]
  ask turtles [ reproduce ]
  ask turtles [ face-death ]
  tick
end

to clear-participation
  ask patches [ set pcolor white ]
  ask turtles [
    set color original-color
    set played? false
  ]
end

to populate
  ask n-of 100 patches [
    sprout 1 [ initialize-agent ]
  ]
end




;; Turtle procedures

to initialize-agent
  set shape "circle"
  set size 0.8
  set original-color blue
  set color original-color
  set score 0
  set played? false
end

to play
  ; Each neighboring pair plays once, rather than once per agent.
  let focal self
  let opponents (turtles-on neighbors4) with [
    who > [who] of focal
  ]

  ask opponents [
    ; Inside this ask, self is the neighbor and focal is the initiator.
    ; 0 = cooperate, 1 = defect; choose independently for each game.
    let my-action random 2
    let their-action random 2
    let my-payoff pd-payoff my-action their-action
    let their-payoff pd-payoff their-action my-action

    receive-payoff my-payoff
    ask focal [ receive-payoff their-payoff ]
  ]
end

to receive-payoff [points]
  set score score + points
  mark-participation
end

to mark-participation
  set played? true
  set color red
end


;; Game rules

to-report pd-payoff [my-action their-action]
  ; Cooperation costs 1 and gives the recipient 3.
  if my-action = 0 and their-action = 0 [ report 2 ]
  if my-action = 0 and their-action = 1 [ report -1 ]
  if my-action = 1 and their-action = 0 [ report 3 ]
  report 0  ; both defect
end
;; Demographic procedures: all agents are eligible, including isolates.

to reproduce
  if random-float 100 < birth-probability [
    let vacancies neighbors4 with [count turtles-here = 0]
    if count vacancies > 0 [
      let destination one-of vacancies
      hatch 1 [
        move-to destination
        set score 0
        set played? false
        set color original-color
      ]
    ]
  ]
end

to face-death
  if random-float 100 < 10 [ die ]
end

;; Observer output procedures

to-report mean-score
  let scores [score] of turtles
  if empty? scores [ report 0 ]
  report mean scores
end

to update-score-histogram
  let scores [score] of turtles
  clear-plot
  if not empty? scores [
    let lowest min scores
    let highest max scores
    set-plot-x-range (lowest - 0.5) (highest + 0.5)
    set-histogram-num-bars (highest - lowest + 1)
    histogram scores
  ]
end

;; Turtle reporter: probability expressed as a percentage.
to-report birth-probability
  report max (list 0 (min (list 100 (12 + score))))
end
]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="10" wrappingAllowedX="true" y="65" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="-16" height="433" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="13.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="true" width="433" tickCounterLabel="ticks" maxPycor="16" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="16" minPxcor="-16"></view>
    <button x="10" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="setup">setup</button>
    <button x="115" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="95" display="go">go</button>
    <button x="220" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="go once">go</button>
    <monitor x="455" precision="3" y="65" height="60" fontSize="12" width="155" display="Mean score">mean-score</monitor>
    <plot x="455" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="Number of agents" y="140" xMin="-5.0" height="350" legend="false" xMax="6.0" yMin="0.0" width="440" xAxis="Score" display="Score histogram">
      <setup></setup>
      <update>update-score-histogram</update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="1" display="agents" color="-13345367" legend="false">
        <setup></setup>
        <update></update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <monitor x="625" precision="0" y="65" height="60" fontSize="12" width="130" display="Population">count turtles</monitor>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## O_2: Random Prisoner's Dilemma with birth and death

### Purpose: changing the game

Replace O_1's RPS game with Prisoner's Dilemma (PD). Agents still identify neighboring partners, accumulate scores, reproduce locally, and face random death. The new game introduces helping at a personal cost. At this stage, every action remains random: there are no group tags, inherited behavioral profiles, memory, or learning.

Yellow patch highlighting is absent in O_2; red agents still mark participation. This display difference does not change interactions or payoffs.

### How to use

Click setup, then go or go once. The run stops at 2000 ticks or when no agents remain. No slider is needed: the stopping condition is written in go.

### Setup and agent states

setup clears the world, colors patches white, calls populate, and initializes CLOCK with reset-ticks. populate selects 100 distinct patches and calls initialize-agent for each new agent. The arena is a 33 by 33 wrapping grid, with at most one agent per patch.

- score: accumulated payoff, initially zero. It persists across ticks and affects birth probability.
- played?: whether an agent played this tick. It resets each tick and does not prevent games with additional neighbors.
- original-color: blue, restored by clear-participation.

Existing agents never relocate. move-to inside hatch places a newborn on its birth patch; the parent stays fixed.

### The go procedure

1. Stop if ticks >= 2000 or count turtles = 0.
2. clear-participation: restore original colors and reset played?, without resetting scores.
3. Select agents with count turtles-on neighbors4 > 0.
4. Ask selected players to play.
5. Ask agents to reproduce.
6. Ask the current population to face-death.
7. tick advances CLOCK and updates outputs.

Filtering players excludes isolates from games only. Isolates can still reproduce and die. Births and deaths change their subsequent neighborhoods.

### Playing with neighboring partners

play finds agents on cardinally adjacent patches, including across wrapping edges. Diagonals and the current patch are excluded. The higher-who comparison ensures each occupied neighboring pair plays once per tick. Every eligible neighbor participates; no single partner is selected with one-of.

Within ask opponents, the active player is the neighbor and focal stores the initiating player. Each independently chooses cooperate (0) or defect (1), with probability one-half per game. pd-payoff calculates the active player's payoff; calling it with reversed actions calculates the other player's payoff. receive-payoff updates each player's score and marks participation.

### Prisoner's Dilemma payoffs

Cooperation costs the actor 1 and gives the recipient 3. Defection provides no help and incurs no giving cost.

| My action | Their action | My payoff | Their payoff |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cooperate | Cooperate | 2 | 2 |
| Cooperate | Defect | -1 | 3 |
| Defect | Cooperate | 3 | -1 |
| Defect | Defect | 0 | 0 |

Defection gives a higher individual payoff against either action, while mutual cooperation benefits both more than mutual defection. This is the dilemma. The game is not zero-sum: one player's payoff is not necessarily the negative of the other's. Under fair independent random choices, expected payoff is 1 per player per game.

### Birth and death

birth-probability reports 12 + cumulative score, bounded to 0-100 percent. reproduce uses that probability for one birth attempt per agent present at the start of the reproduction stage. A successful attempt also requires an empty neighbors4 patch. If none exists, no offspring is born. The parent's score is not spent.

An offspring starts at score zero, with played? false and the parent's original color. There are no different inherited playing strategies yet. Newborns do not play or reproduce until the next tick.

face-death gives every current agent, including newborns, an independent 10% death chance. Death is unrelated to score. Death follows reproduction, so newly vacated patches are available for future ticks.

### Outputs and supporting procedures

mark-participation sets played? true and the player color to red. Patches remain white. Newborns start blue.

The Mean score monitor calls mean-score, an observer reporter that collects surviving agents' scores and reports their mean. It reports zero as a display placeholder on extinction.

The plot calls update-score-histogram, which clears the old distribution and uses one bin per integer score. When the population is empty, the histogram stays empty. Named output procedures avoid complex widget expressions and use the output structure tested in NetLogo Web for O_1.

Population reports count turtles. The mean is not constrained to zero, and it can rise as agents accumulate positive payoffs. Population turnover also changes the mean. No score is a probability, and scores can fall below zero or exceed 100.

### What O_2 adds

O_1 demonstrates demographic consequences of random competition. O_2 introduces the cooperation-exploitation tension while retaining its encounter and demographic rules. The model does not yet select between different inherited cooperation profiles; all agents choose randomly. Those profiles arrive in O_3. Scores continue accumulating here; O_4 later introduces a per-tick reset.

### Relation to Hammond–Axelrod

Cost 1 and benefit 3 scale the paper's increments 0.01 and 0.03 by 100. The 12% birth baseline, 10% random death, and 2000-period horizon borrow their baseline values. Using cumulative scores and random actions is our teaching step, not a replication of HA's inherited conditional strategies and per-cycle reproductive reset. There is no mutation or immigration here.

Source: Hammond and Axelrod (2006), The Evolution of Ethnocentrism, manuscript pp. 3-4 and 8-9: https://websites.umich.edu/~axe/research/Hammond%20and%20Axelrod%20JCR%2006.pdf

### Credits

Professor Jose Manuel MAGALLANES  
Complexity and ABM, 2026  
DACSS - UMASS
]]></info>
